From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:29:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/14] media: apple: vxd: find and parse H.264 slices in the driver

The device runs in V4L2_STATELESS_H264_DECODE_MODE_FRAME_BASED with
Annex-B start codes.  In that mode the OUTPUT buffer carries the whole
access unit and V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_SLICE_PARAMS is not used, but the
MSVDX command stream still needs per-slice values: where the slice NAL
is (the bitstream DMA), how many bits of header precede slice_data()
(the SR_SETUP bit offset), the slice type, first macroblock, QP and
deblocking parameters, and the reference-list modifications.  The kext
gets them from its own NAL walk (HOST_H264DecodeFrame); so does this
file.

apple_vxd_h264_find_slices() walks the access unit, skips parameter
sets, SEI and the like, drops redundant slices, refuses data
partitioning, ASO and slices that disagree on the picture, and parses
clause 7.3.3 for each slice NAL with an emulation-prevention-aware bit
reader.  The header length is reported in RBSP bits and in bits of the
escaped byte stream, which is what the core's shift register counts
(it strips the emulation-prevention bytes itself).
apple_vxd_h264_modify_ref_list() applies clause 8.2.4.3 for frames to an
initial list built by the v4l2-h264 helper.

Scope: progressive frames, one slice group, no separate colour planes.
The file uses no allocation, lock or MMIO, so the host harness builds it
unchanged.

Signed-off-by: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <maintainer@example.invalid>
